---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on router1 (AS 1): the eBGP neighbor statement for
193.10.11.2 (router2) has been removed from the FRR daemon's
running configuration while the session was live, but the
on-disk config (/etc/frr/frr.conf) still contains the correct
entry "neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2". This creates a split
between the file and in-memory state.

When the neighbor was deconfigured in the running BGP process,
FRR immediately sent a BGP NOTIFICATION message to router2 with
code "Cease / Peer De-configured", tearing down the established
session. Router2 received the NOTIFICATION (confirmed in its
neighbor detail: "Connections established 1; dropped 1, Last
reset Notification received Cease/Peer De-configured/Hard
Reset"), transitioned to Active state, and has been retrying
every 120 seconds since. Router1, having no neighbor configured
in memory, silently rejects each incoming TCP SYN on port 179
from 193.10.11.2 — the session never re-establishes.

Without a BGP session, neither router exchanges prefixes:
router1 does not install a route to 200.1.1.0/24 and router2
does not install a route to 195.11.14.0/24, so pc1↔pc2
traffic has no forwarding path beyond the directly-connected
segments.

Root cause: Someone executed "no neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2"
(or equivalent) on router1 via vtysh, stripping the neighbor from
the live BGP process without saving, leaving frr.conf intact but
the daemon misconfigured.

Corrective fix: On router1, re-enter the neighbor into the running
configuration with:
  vtysh -c "conf t" -c "router bgp 1" -c "neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2"
or simply reload FRR so it re-reads /etc/frr/frr.conf:
  systemctl reload frr
The session will re-establish within one BGP Connect Retry interval
and route exchange will resume automatically.
=== END ===
```